Memory-intensive sorting, three partitions
==========================================

A memory-intensive test: random number generation followed by sorting, so that
memory accesses are the performance bottleneck.

What it tests
-------------

How the RTOS handles memory-intensive applications in separate partitions
("unmixed": each partition runs only this kind of load).

What it does
------------

Three partitions, P1, P2 and P3, each with two periodic processes.

Each partition has a generator process (GEN_A653, 10 ms budget) that fills an
array with random numbers, and a sorting process (SORT_A653) that sorts it:
bubble sort in P1 and P2, insertion sort in P3. Windows: P1 1300 ms, P2 1200
ms, P3 2200 ms in a 4700 ms major frame; both processes have a 4700 ms period.

Expected output
---------------

"GENERATED, WAITING", "BUBBLE SORTED" / "INSERT SORTED" and the execution-time
statistics of each process.

Notes
-----

The generator has a hard 10 ms time capacity. In QEMU its first activation
after a (re)start sometimes takes longer (code translation), and the partition
is restarted for a missed deadline.

Build and run
-------------

From the top-level directory (builds every benchmark; QEMU needs only the one):

    make benchmarks                     # x86_64
    make BSP=arm64-qemu benchmarks      # arm64

    qemu-system-x86_64 -vga none -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/MEM_SORT_UNMIXED_MONO/build/x86_64-qemu/pok.elf
    qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a53 -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/MEM_SORT_UNMIXED_MONO/build/arm64-qemu/pok.elf

To build only this benchmark, run "scons bsp=x86_64-qemu" (or bsp=arm64-qemu)
in this directory with POK_PATH set. The benchmark runs forever; quit QEMU with
Ctrl-A X.
